uuid "9f8f8710-c311-53fb-8e05-09ef68f11bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258188} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a01c21-7c97-554c-b4cc-4f0b9551d88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258189} {:answer/id #uuid "2dd8e7b9-989a-565a-989d-3b186c2d6b3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258190} {:answer/id #uuid "2e40f366-546c-52fe-86b2-d49a6d72fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258191} {:answer/id #uuid "2180a959-e016-537a-92ef-d275bfc73a59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258192} {:answer/id #uuid "26c0d665-c3af-5a56-9f89-85e80babc162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e40f366-546c-52fe-86b2-d49a6d72fa74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258191} :answer-field/id #uuid "0925d6eb-4b1e-563a-b56e-57bf1b55bfe0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258185}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4be75c59-4ccf-5cca-98bb-74c5dc036895" :question/math-academy-id "q-143194" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=4{n}^{6}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=4{n}^{6}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(n+1)^{6}}{4{n}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{6}}{{n}^{6}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}^{6} \\\\  & ={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ we have that $\\frac{1}{n}>0.$ Therefore, it follows that \n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{1}{n})>1,\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\nL={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{6}>1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90b485f8-a07d-55f4-8cde-f699070eeae7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Monotonic Polynomial and Rational Sequences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9faf36f-d5c0-5429-8740-32d1a5c32e66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258196} {:answer/id #uuid "3d21c5dd-cc0b-5b2c-b6e1-64843eed4823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258197} {:answer/id #uuid "e2902026-1882-5969-b07f-1402cf2309a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258198} {:answer/id #uuid "36fc3e07-67bd-5f21-90c6-f99647d25d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258199} {:answer/id #uuid "29cb7f46-285c-5637-b3c8-4ddf6730bc05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258200} {:answer/id #uuid "b6435d47-f7bf-5948-a941-0de3a9f5cafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258201} {:answer/id #uuid "7433c384-9613-5f54-a21a-f194e850efe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258202} {:answer/id #uuid "ccaad856-45db-5d10-8730-1a550595f04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccaad856-45db-5d10-8730-1a550595f04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258203} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ebfaf26-9321-5379-bb3f-c467ba5f5f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3210b6e-fd27-5b2e-a30b-59e4d6036658" :question/math-academy-id "q-143195" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=7{n}^{4}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=7{n}^{4}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7(n+1)^{4}}{7{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{4}}{{n}^{4}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n+1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{n}{n}+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4} \\\\  & ={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ we have that $\\frac{1}{n}>0.$ Therefore, it follows that\n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{1}{n})>1,\n$$\n\nand therefore \n\n$$\nL={(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4}>1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true whereas statement III is false. Since $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da6bc3cc-0232-5dd8-9427-892582464a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)-1,\\sqrt2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271090} {:answer/id #uuid "2824e57a-1834-5ee6-a083-55e05b80436b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271091} {:answer/id #uuid "554b84a5-294c-564c-a583-7434c77d6807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)+2,\\sqrt2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271092} {:answer/id #uuid "dad9532a-c57a-59c1-b4ab-ca8377528fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)-2,\\sqrt2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271093} {:answer/id #uuid "279ac03a-6b85-595b-af67-7b91e3776533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{(-\\sqrt2)+1,\\sqrt2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2824e57a-1834-5ee6-a083-55e05b80436b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\{-\\sqrt2,\\sqrt2\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271091} :answer-field/id #uuid "50fd0980-ca42-52a0-8dc0-fea4a1971144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274494}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fa079b5f-e6f6-5243-ab92-5790c7d64511" :question/math-academy-id "q-156159" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, what is the condition for the real constant $K$ guaranteeing that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-{K}^{2})\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is **convergent?**" :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nHowever, multiplying the terms of a divergent series by $0$ gives a series that converges to $0.$\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(2-{K}^{2})\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-{K}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {K}^{2} & =2 \\\\ K & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea24c558-8664-5b22-bdfc-71189a45a360" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a25ad8-e29b-56a3-b192-76993c77364f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271123} {:answer/id #uuid "a4dc02b3-dc50-51c1-8e07-05b4672b1e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271124} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b391e1-90ed-546b-8e26-9e9f06e22d55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K < 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271125} {:answer/id #uuid "e498c74f-a758-5cd0-971b-f3ab083ed10d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271126} {:answer/id #uuid "adb18a37-1e50-52ac-9090-0d013154ff7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a25ad8-e29b-56a3-b192-76993c77364f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271123} :answer-field/id #uuid "f467f414-4f2b-5bd0-a78a-e7905b77c6da" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274501}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a172fe0-8627-5d13-a398-073dac6cbf1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-156876" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which $\\sqrt{K}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent." :question/worked-solution "Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series. \n\nHowever, the expression $\\sqrt{K}$ is not defined when $K<0.$ \n\nTherefore, the series  \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{K}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis convergent for $K\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d98bcee-d473-53b1-a6ef-1943d68702c3" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Divergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dd6cfef-aa3f-5794-b506-afbe2720ae66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271128} {:answer/id #uuid "ecc21a7d-4ec0-581d-b2f5-7aa4e1679ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271129} {:answer/id #uuid "8927ed96-fb26-50bd-957f-744683335021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,-1]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271130} {:answer/id #uuid "62c694ce-5904-5f3e-99c5-122e81243115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{K}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271131} {:answer/id #uuid "553dac70-9f4c-53fc-b5ac-18b0585d24c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62c694ce-5904-5f3e-99c5-122e81243115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{K}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271131} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8404262-db31-5a76-b11c-dd1fe1736537" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274502}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b57d0b6-bc38-5056-bac4-445ec8a4f7ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-156904" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent, determine all real values of $K$ for which ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({K}^{3}+1)\\cdot {a}_{n}$ is divergent." :question/worked-solution "If we multiply the terms of a divergent series by a non-zero constant, the resulting series is divergent.\n\nTherefore, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({K}^{3}+1)\\cdot {a}_{n}\n$$\n\nis divergent when\n\n$$\n{K}^{3}+1\\ne 0\\,\\Rightarrow \\,K\\ne -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1026f31e-5989-50a0-8fbd-756f26e8ef1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271200} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a57807-2a5e-5767-91a6-ccee3e462292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271201} {:answer/id #uuid "8d57df3b-f8dc-57be-81c0-150e6200f128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271202} {:answer/id #uuid "08143c7d-7e2c-5b2b-967c-78d8b2935d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271203} {:answer/id #uuid "00f91f2d-4dfc-5765-9eb6-d23953e8c168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08143c7d-7e2c-5b2b-967c-78d8b2935d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271203} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d92c01-7358-5906-9a67-fc86d331d073" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274518}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cea81d37-e8da-5ed2-8186-38548c31655d" :question/math-academy-id "q-16699" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(1)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{3}(5{x}^{2}+2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{3},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=3{u}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=10x.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{2}+2))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2))\\cdot 10x \\\\  & =-30x{cos}^{2}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{2}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we compute ${f}^{'}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(1) & =-30(1){cos}^{2}(5(1)^{2}+2)\\operatorname{sin}(5(1)^{2}+2) \\\\  & =-30{cos}^{2}7\\operatorname{sin}7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "504d5c88-6749-54d6-915b-12a8cb7c3184" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x\\tan x − 2\\sin x − 3\\sec^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271320} {:answer/id #uuid "735c0fcc-22c3-527f-894e-60c667579ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271321} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8b45ba-8ec0-59ec-9900-45978733a875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x\\tan x − 2\\cos x − 3\\sec^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271322} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce1abcb-1065-598b-a77e-4faaa9eb94ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc^{2} x − 2\\cos x − 3\\sec^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271323} {:answer/id #uuid "e3722595-aa9d-5141-bcad-024f17627431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x\\tan x − 2\\cos x − 3\\csc^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "735c0fcc-22c3-527f-894e-60c667579ea0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271321} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b5fb529-5b93-5cda-bddd-4ebcb5afa65d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274542}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49490cb9-3ce1-5534-a5d6-1fffc1caa530" :question/math-academy-id "q-1883" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-2}+\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{-3}+(-\\operatorname{sin}x)-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\  & =-6{x}^{-3}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{{x}^{3}}-\\operatorname{sin}x-3\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "54834980-1e19-58eb-b15f-e46e2a1aeffe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unknown Parameters Given a Stretched Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8564bfa3-81ce-50ed-92ac-d11ef6903b40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271345} {:answer/id #uuid "bc007d23-f8f2-546b-af27-cfa144041a8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271346} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2b4b8f-860b-5852-89e9-4f199fbd9aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271347} {:answer/id #uuid "052c6427-3e1d-5960-a193-5d33aef56137" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271348} {:answer/id #uuid "16316609-d2b5-53d9-bc49-336462a06264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16316609-d2b5-53d9-bc49-336462a06264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271349} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fae57d2-24af-5bf8-97b8-159ccd4f7538" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274547}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "075f5bd8-8f8e-5a44-8865-d55c0e9f72f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-19262" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/25e78d632f4bbe23ebbf700946b1a05a6b4f9f578c1d8047ddb067842a820fa9.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}x,$ and the point ${A}^{'}$ with coordinates $(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(6,\\operatorname{ln}6)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 6,\\operatorname{ln}6)=(4,\\operatorname{ln}6)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53816514-3d9b-500b-aaf6-c8d7b00cdd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271355} {:answer/id #uuid "c15e3345-7d60-5694-805c-6fd0c84fc13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x\\tan x − 2\\sin x − 3\\sec^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271356} {:answer/id #uuid "f60ae0f4-5085-590f-b581-80832dd8212b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x\\tan x − 2\\cos x − 3\\sec^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271357} {:answer/id #uuid "48d90591-5b57-5a49-a361-8d9cab5bc627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc^{2} x − 2\\cos x − 3\\sec^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271358} {:answer/id #uuid "83b01e4b-93c3-53bc-99ea-3f97108398c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x\\tan x − 2\\cos x − 3\\csc^{2} x + 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53816514-3d9b-500b-aaf6-c8d7b00cdd6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271355} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3afd37d-b034-5b6d-870d-e0519f7871fd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274549}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "124929ec-0b47-501a-8761-14e3174bc1cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1938" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}x}{4x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x)(4x)-(\\operatorname{sec}x)(4)}{(4x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-4\\operatorname{sec}x}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x)}{16{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sec}x\\operatorname{tan}x-\\operatorname{sec}x}{4{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2684f4ec-ad3a-54a7-9948-4fba58d8ecc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(30x)/((3x + 5)^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271508} {:answer/id #uuid "7d775ce0-7461-5ebd-aa67-57e4b7e49139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271509} {:answer/id #uuid "235b0134-f577-5653-9d72-a1dc432023cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5x)/((3x + 5)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271510} {:answer/id #uuid "95352f66-6bd5-5c2c-a253-effed617e7e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x)/((3x + 5)^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271511} {:answer/id #uuid "1da2ff63-2357-5d9a-a7b1-28130880cdf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6x)/((3x + 5)^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d775ce0-7461-5ebd-aa67-57e4b7e49139" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271509} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe1fbe7-475d-5fde-9ab5-8e6c5ec6a621" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274579}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7da6583b-f41d-5231-9e7b-686d369d4d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-2400" :question/problem "Find ${y}^{'}$ for $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{{x}^{3}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{3}+2.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=\\frac{3}{x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{x})({x}^{3}+2)-(\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3}))(3{x}^{2})}{({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}^{3}+2)-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}({x}^{3})+6}{x({x}^{3}+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03bf73f-e8ce-5f5b-9004-f803d8be9067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4\\pi$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b03bf73f-e8ce-5f5b-9004-f803d8be9067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4\\pi$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259408} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a47021-fb80-56ac-9acd-3b46f185f95e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4007a637-1203-5eab-9c3e-d86e260889f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-310182" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $y =\\sin \\left(\\pi \\left(x^{2} + 2x - 2\\right)\\right)$ at $x = 1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b371fb-b274-5d68-beaa-c397249ac84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(24)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271764} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc0720c-db0c-59d3-a913-d24e4a3271ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(24)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271765} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd8037d-f55b-5a48-ada5-b2e379740f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(24)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271766} {:answer/id #uuid "b0dd6b9b-6ed1-5e55-8fd3-0e383ddea8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(24)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271767} {:answer/id #uuid "00b58038-663c-505d-aa68-b7259115eb14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00b58038-663c-505d-aa68-b7259115eb14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271768} :answer-field/id #uuid "af383e6a-4928-57f5-8f5f-1f41c739f504" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274632}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "81fa46b9-6e38-5bbb-a82c-d73296a4d0fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-31288" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ if $f(x)=-6\\operatorname{cot}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we just take the constant out of the derivative and use the result for the derivative of $\\operatorname{cot}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =-6(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =6{csc}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =6{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =6{(2)}^{2} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3ee0d5d-0821-5f6b-b613-6b814e65df50" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using Multiple Instances of the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01a74bc4-19e8-59d6-b3c4-1f7913db2ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01a74bc4-19e8-59d6-b3c4-1f7913db2ec0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259435} :answer-field/id #uuid "36db61fa-c70d-5a2d-8faa-8f54dc214ad6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259434}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6422b1f8-1755-59dd-a7bc-a54ffd2688c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-312907" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ when $f(x)={cos}^{8}(5{x}^{3}).$\n\n${f}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}} ${cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3})$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={u}^{8},\\,u=\\operatorname{cos}v,\\,v=5{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=8{u}^{7},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}=-\\operatorname{sin}v,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=15{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nApplying now the chain rule twice, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}v}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =8{u}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}v)\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8{(\\operatorname{cos}(5{x}^{3}))}^{7}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}))\\cdot 15{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-120{x}^{2}{cos}^{7}(5{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}(5{x}^{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef890421-0d33-5455-a70b-afc3dbc58610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24x^{2} + 1)\\sec^{2} (8x^{3} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271769} {:answer/id #uuid "1dab1e87-38c1-515b-a0df-5d03ce050c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24x^{2}\\sec^{2} (8x^{3} + 1)\\tan (8x^{3} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271770} {:answer/id #uuid "613b9bef-afca-5514-81f6-b59b30075178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24x^{2}\\tan (8x^{3} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271771} {:answer/id #uuid "446d1f8f-aeee-5888-8028-c45d7434c64c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(24x^{2} + 1)\\sec (8x^{3} + 1)\\tan (8x^{3} + 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271772} {:answer/id #uuid "2df50811-29d3-544b-a186-f20ae1c09341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\csc(10x)\\cot(10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2df50811-29d3-544b-a186-f20ae1c09341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80\\csc(10x)\\cot(10x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271773} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3856518-4836-5fae-847d-c0aac6b202ce" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274633}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01c41333-1844-5900-ad46-7ce70f99f944" :question/math-academy-id "q-31333" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ for $y =8\\csc(10x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = 8\\csc{u}, \\qquad u = 10x\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -8\\csc u \\cot u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 10.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-8\\csc u \\cot u \\cdot (10)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-80\\csc u \\cot u \\\\[5pt]\n&=-80\\csc (10x) \\cot (10x).\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5b8f9fb-c244-5126-9181-8847430c5aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5b8f9fb-c244-5126-9181-8847430c5aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259444} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd475635-649d-56fe-b8a0-c5c13f9d210c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259443}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a22f8807-f06b-5bb5-ba16-a2214209db89" :question/math-academy-id "q-313362" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(3x+5)^{2}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${(3x+5)}^{3}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)={x}^{3}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=3{x}^{2},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3{x}^{2}){(3x+5)}^{2}-({x}^{3})(6(3x+5))}{{({(3x+5)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}{(3x+5)}^{2}-6{x}^{3}(3x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(3x+5)((3x+5)-2x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+5)}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdf37534-ad6d-5de4-8935-78d88d0ef843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271774} {:answer/id #uuid "31e2e3cc-8abb-5650-9a3b-09cbed626d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271775} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8ffc66-86d0-5669-9f77-a0c0f9a5a19c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271776} {:answer/id #uuid "75235109-51d3-5929-9152-c776018d603e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271777} {:answer/id #uuid "a617d959-c350-569b-8066-9df244c34d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a617d959-c350-569b-8066-9df244c34d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac15\\csc^2(\\frac45)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271778} :answer-field/id #uuid "056b23e7-5a1d-5bfb-bc72-a9301e02b63a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83184978-b220-5676-82cf-9ec259139917" :question/math-academy-id "q-31341" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\cot\\left(1-\\dfrac x 5 \\right)$ at the point where $x =1.$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cot{u}, \\qquad u = 1-\\dfrac x 5.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc^2 u \\qquad \\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\csc^2 u \\cdot  \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2 \\left(1-\\dfrac{x}{5}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n    \\eqalign{\n\nf'(x) & =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(1-\\dfrac 1 5 \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t& =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac 4 5 \\right) .\n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is $\\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b335a3e-c923-596c-aa29-0ddd5d423979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271779} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b488fa-a83a-5915-90f7-796ffb2db9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271780} {:answer/id #uuid "3262d936-0bc0-54d8-baa5-31a140c9c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271781} {:answer/id #uuid "c58626c7-939c-5c0f-b636-aba013231816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271782} {:answer/id #uuid "0bdc0d3b-fb53-504d-987e-2379366cf00e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3262d936-0bc0-54d8-baa5-31a140c9c6f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271781} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f362c67-575c-5b3a-9987-41fa44176c83" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274635}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e90a75e5-83c6-52f3-9c5a-0f176678967d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31355" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{'}(x)$ if $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{csc}^{2}x)\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{cot}x\\cdot (2x)}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-x{csc}^{2}x-2\\operatorname{cot}x}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbb56f0a-ead0-5794-9e33-7fe0e1f87dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c318c7-19c4-55b9-ad35-06d42b8bf435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4c318c7-19c4-55b9-ad35-06d42b8bf435" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259456} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad46b0f-dbd5-54af-9929-d948ec59e431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259455}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ba46c76-d873-5609-acab-7d3bfa67173b" :question/math-academy-id "q-313722" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{'}(x)$ if $h(x)=4x\\operatorname{csc}(x).$\n\n${h}^{'}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)) \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{csc}(x))+(4x)(-\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{csc}(x)-4x\\operatorname{csc}(x)\\operatorname{cot}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8eebff2-97ca-599b-a819-04ff34f0c1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Exponential Equations Containing Different Bases by Rewriting Two Exponents"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0306a2da-b7a4-58f7-a3ff-9067f15a091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271854} {:answer/id #uuid "f5269bf8-07f7-58b8-b2b5-d2d2353f633b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271855} {:answer/id #uuid "dd2f1f59-d1b3-50f3-acf9-b1eb7ff8610c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271856} {:answer/id #uuid "9beb736f-0893-5acf-9840-93349096df31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271857} {:answer/id #uuid "3584ec70-0821-5977-87af-d0fc6ab166c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd2f1f59-d1b3-50f3-acf9-b1eb7ff8610c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271856} :answer-field/id #uuid "97363cb2-e70c-5023-94f0-bce1711aefe1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274650}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11121e1d-3771-5b73-8d84-a5287eac4550" :question/math-academy-id "q-3362" :question/problem "If ${27}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{81})}^{-2n+8},$ then $n=$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $27={3}^{3}$ and $81={3}^{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{3})}^{2n+2}={(\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}})}^{-2n+8}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the power of quotient rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}=\\frac{1}{{3}^{4(-2n+8)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3(2n+2)}={3}^{-4(-2n+8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2n+2) & =-4(-2n+8) \\\\ 6n+6 & =8n-32 \\\\ 2n & =38 \\\\ n & =19\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e4b3974-1288-524e-a34c-8091c0e072f1" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Secant Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc4dd606-3245-5362-863c-9cd43490bd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc4dd606-3245-5362-863c-9cd43490bd4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259783} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0acb996-b153-55cf-bbe7-8329594e8e99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259782}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b58612b7-225f-5023-841c-cc50f36c7d9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-337182" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{2}}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{3}}($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\,(\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sec}(x)\\operatorname{tan}(x))(5{x}^{2})-(3\\operatorname{sec}(x))(10x)}{(5{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}(x)\\operatorname{tan}(x)-30x\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{25{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x\\operatorname{sec}(x)(3x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-6)}{25{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(x)(3x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-6)}{5{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sec}(x)}{5{x}^{3}}(x\\operatorname{tan}(x)-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73911311-b113-5748-be58-21901c884257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73911311-b113-5748-be58-21901c884257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259904} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcc7f983-afa9-534d-9d90-007ddaf612bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259903}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c556fe2-0de3-5c83-a8df-8864a240ec1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340642" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $y=g(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ if $g(x)=\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the product rule and sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{csc}x\\operatorname{cot}x)(\\operatorname{cot}x)+(\\operatorname{csc}x)(-{csc}^{2}x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}x({cot}^{2}x+{csc}^{2}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and evaluate the slope $m:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={g}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{csc}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})[{cot}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{csc}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot ({0}^{2}+{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a13c5fa3-74bc-547e-aafc-cf53b0e2a85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a13c5fa3-74bc-547e-aafc-cf53b0e2a85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259977} :answer-field/id #uuid "13abd639-5f6a-515e-9363-f08635a47382" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259976}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28984f6e-c237-5f01-8863-8027f6429ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-342405" :question/problem "Find the slope $m$ of $f(x) = \\sec\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by $f'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. We express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\sec{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec{u}\\tan{u}, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\pi x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\sec{u}\\tan{u} \\cdot 2\\pi x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 2\\pi x \\sec{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}\\tan{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we find \n\\begin{eqnarray}\n \\eqalign{\nf'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) &= 2\\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sec{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]}\\tan{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\sec{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi\\sqrt{2}.}\n\\end{eqnarray}\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb0d5e7-3cfa-5971-a047-f58f04ee2b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\pi\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddb0d5e7-3cfa-5971-a047-f58f04ee2b47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-\\pi\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259980} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd9790b-7e42-5e1b-98ac-44ebce2a0ea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259979} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f88db24-9e14-5959-aa53-c08754c1eb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{3}+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f88db24-9e14-5959-aa53-c08754c1eb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\pi\\sqrt{3}+2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259982} :answer-field/id #uuid "6416b67c-840b-5049-a1c1-7e1b0c065c89" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259981}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c48ab644-069c-576e-8de1-e3fed8004930" :question/math-academy-id "q-342412" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x) = \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}$ at the point $(1,2).$\n\n$y =$\n{{field-1}}$x \\: + \\:$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot{u}  \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(-\\csc{u}\\cot{u}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\\begin{align}\nm=f'(1)& =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\[5pt] \n\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -\\pi\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{align}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,2\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny-2 &= -\\pi\\sqrt{3}(x-1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny &= \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-\\pi\\sqrt{3}}x + \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{3} + 2}\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7d80b1-259c-5bc4-ba87-8ab1f5ca7b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d7d80b1-259c-5bc4-ba87-8ab1f5ca7b4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259985} :answer-field/id #uuid "be1c0972-ec5c-5c46-800f-b7700c4cafca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259984}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c59f39-d500-5872-99f5-af5db1f70149" :question/math-academy-id "q-342413" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = -2\\sin^{3}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = -2(\\sin{(x)})^{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = -2u^{3}, \\qquad u = \\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm df}{\\textrm d u} = -6u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -6u^2 \\cdot \\cos{(x)}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}}.\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a849f73b-fdf5-55c0-a59f-c2ca004c1be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a849f73b-fdf5-55c0-a59f-c2ca004c1be9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-7\\cos^{6}{(x)}\\sin{(x)}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259988} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0a176b-9600-5544-8caa-1de4b7d6c542" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259987}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c25b5ade-5d47-5a5f-b5a6-b2a486a4775d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342427" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\cos^{7}{(x)},$ we have\n\n$f'(x) = $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = (\\cos{(x)})^{7}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = u^{7}, \\qquad u = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm d u} = 7u^6, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 7u^6 \\cdot (-\\sin{(x)})\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-7\\cos^6{(x)}\\sin{(x)}}.\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "056fe1ad-11a3-5c3e-8d31-5680255cf264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056fe1ad-11a3-5c3e-8d31-5680255cf264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260006} :answer-field/id #uuid "32b5ed67-b70b-53e3-b2ab-9bb336284e9a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b225130-6791-5c02-b157-558955b95349" :question/math-academy-id "q-343333" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={e}^{5x}\\operatorname{ln}x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=($\n{{field-1}} $){e}^{5x}$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{5x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{'}(x)=5{e}^{5x},\\,{v}^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =(5{e}^{5x})(\\operatorname{ln}x)+({e}^{5x})\\,(\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(5\\operatorname{ln}x+\\frac{1}{x}){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da596db-98ab-5d0a-9727-83ec6f492af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da596db-98ab-5d0a-9727-83ec6f492af3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260073} :answer-field/id #uuid "24c737f4-f34c-5872-9cb2-c62d52005703" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260072}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6feb18f4-8454-57e9-a1f1-b1f2a3e71478" :question/math-academy-id "q-347372" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}=\\frac{10}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}{5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0adffc7-ef35-5f7a-a790-06addb354c2e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum to Infinity of a Geometric Series Given Two of Its Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2bde024-7ec9-51c5-97d1-87f23fbc0040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bde024-7ec9-51c5-97d1-87f23fbc0040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260079} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1d16ffa-4c18-506b-81a9-7b7cd5ff8c61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260078}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3b81faf4-0bdd-52be-b70d-34db9528cb79" :question/math-academy-id "q-347774" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a fourth term equal to $16$ and a seventh term equal to $\\frac{16}{125}.$ Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the terms of this sequence. Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{4}=16$ and ${a}_{7}=\\frac{16}{125}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{125})}{16} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{16}{125})(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=4$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{3} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\ 16 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{125}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =2000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =2000(\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =2500.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cfe5a9ca-d045-53ae-bc69-fff9dfbdc439" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Partial Sum of an Infinite Arithmetic Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b1478c6-3c7e-5da2-b053-0af134795669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(3-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b1478c6-3c7e-5da2-b053-0af134795669" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k(3-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260106} :answer-field/id #uuid "33ad890a-0dcc-5b5c-a985-2f7327b87568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260105}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "04a861e6-bb06-5ca0-a8f9-ed37e1946328" :question/math-academy-id "q-349085" :question/problem "Find the $k$th partial sum ${s}_{k}$ of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4-2n).$ Express the answer in terms of the parameter $k.$\n\n${s}_{k}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4-2n.$ If we compute the first few terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=-2$ and ${a}_{1}=2.$\n\nWe can compute the $k$th partial sum of an arithmetic series using the formula\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}=\\frac{k}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{k}).\n$$\n\nSince ${a}_{k}=4-2k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{k} & =\\frac{k}{2}(2+4-2k) \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{2}(6-2k) \\\\  & =k(3-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9cd8e5-27e2-58d9-8af9-f42c3800d9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260132}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9cd8e5-27e2-58d9-8af9-f42c3800d9d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260132} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e54e373-cf38-5ad3-9996-f860bc1db291" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260131}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f8f961d-0c12-55d8-a6b7-610d52702173" :question/math-academy-id "q-350588" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(0.2)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=3{(0.2)}^{1}=0.6.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{3{(0.2)}^{2}}{3{(0.2)}^{1}}=0.2.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(0.2)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{1-0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{0.8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a09274-3b82-53bb-92cc-b7199d2b2359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a09274-3b82-53bb-92cc-b7199d2b2359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260135} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b831cea-f9fe-5289-ab8e-950cf1dfb3ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260134}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ecd963bf-5bba-5df2-996d-756760d0d41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-350592" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{1}=0.42.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{2}}{\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{1}}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{5}{(0.3)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.42}{1-0.3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.42}{0.7} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{70} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5191b207-da9d-5408-8efd-3c616ffd3211" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f810e291-0c36-56a6-a2a9-94abdfc6f33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f810e291-0c36-56a6-a2a9-94abdfc6f33f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260138} :answer-field/id #uuid "20d1584c-c343-5091-b5fb-8a3f42a0ea37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260137}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a570aa2-6317-52cf-88ac-f33d0dd4de74" :question/math-academy-id "q-350593" :question/problem "Evaluate the following series. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(0.75)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given series is a geometric series because it is in the form\n$\\sum a{r}^{n}.$\n\nThe sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nThe first term is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6{(0.75)}^{1}=4.5.\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{6{(0.75)}^{2}}{6{(0.75)}^{1}}=0.75.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(0.75)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{1-0.75} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.5}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{450}{25} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260145 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "530b0b21-6349-5ce4-acd2-d20f4c6d7b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Infinite Geometric Series Expressed in Non-Standard Form Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a15f53-0b90-53fe-809b-00e1e651cdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77a15f53-0b90-53fe-809b-00e1e651cdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260147} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6c0036-bfd5-5b86-9819-1c43e8908c53" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a515033-9d33-5db6-a4a5-02cf6fa471fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-350633" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n+1}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2+1}=\\frac{27}{5}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3+1}=\\frac{81}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{81}{25})}{(\\frac{27}{5})}=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }25{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{5})}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{27}{5})}{(\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f975e5c-637e-57f6-a193-68254799e79b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Sum of Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7d861e0-b37e-5859-8382-862845635d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7d861e0-b37e-5859-8382-862845635d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260162} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2db9c67-a917-5a3d-8f0f-9bfae2afb4b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260161}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fa440b09-fed9-5ae8-94fe-6509db1fed32" :question/math-academy-id "q-352290" :question/problem "Calculate \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{b}_{n}$\ngiven that \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}=2$ and \n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{b}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Taking the constant factors outside of the summation signs gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4{b}_{n} & =3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c42e3765-d50b-52a5-9b7d-eab3fdb27b9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272325} {:answer/id #uuid "da106825-3d50-5fd4-875b-0225ec90b3bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272326} {:answer/id #uuid "c6fd4156-cdf4-5d52-ac1a-1ee16c97c31c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272327} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2b7fc9-ba08-5d99-9f2a-3b8557f45d18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272328} {:answer/id #uuid "dd732e61-ef50-58fb-b84a-5d40e3acf694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd732e61-ef50-58fb-b84a-5d40e3acf694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272329} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf18eeb0-a3c1-53ba-9d17-3faa3f37aba2" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274745}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9b3f537-d998-5b44-ac6e-d3ede45445e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-48508" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y = \\cos (5x-2 )$ at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5,1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac 2 5$ is given by $f'\\left(\\dfrac 2 5\\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = 5x-2.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot 5\\\\[5pt]\n&=-5\\sin u\\\\[5pt]\n&= -5\\sin\\left(5x-2 \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac 25,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n \\eqalign{\n\nm=f'\\left(\\dfrac 25\\right)  & =- 5 \\sin \\left(5\\cdot \\dfrac 2 5-2\\right)\\\\[5pt] \n\t\t& =- 5 \\sin 0\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t&= 0.\n \n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(\\dfrac 2 5,1\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\ny-1&=0\\left(x-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\ny-1&=0\\\\[5pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align*}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1854ceaa-5f83-5e47-83ed-8e709c4e2047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(-2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272330} {:answer/id #uuid "e30c8150-48e7-5289-bcc9-e8e610cecc86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}(-2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272331} {:answer/id #uuid "0d741453-d199-582d-bb23-bb277c562160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272332} {:answer/id #uuid "11d2c1d7-7e36-5676-afba-eafe5779a5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-(-2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272333} {:answer/id #uuid "894e5253-c977-5517-9843-c4ff957ec28d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(-2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d741453-d199-582d-bb23-bb277c562160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272332} :answer-field/id #uuid "796a1d10-7f1c-59d8-bcbc-6e17817193df" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5d322c8f-a329-5374-8649-0dbb3acf87eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48510" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sec(3x)-2x$ at the point $\\left(0,1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=\\sec(3x )$ and $h(x)=-2x.$ Then,\n\n\\begin{align}\nf(x)&=g(x)+h(x) . \n\\end{align}\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\sec(3x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\ng = \\sec u, \\qquad u = 3x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d g}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec u \\tan u \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\\require{cancel}\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}g}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}g}{\\textrm{d}u}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\sec u \\tan u \\cdot (3)\\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=3 \\sec u \\tan u\\\\[5pt]\n                                                                        & =3\\sec (3x) \\tan (3x)\n}\n\n\\]\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-2x.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d x} = -2\n\\]\n\nHence,\n\\[f'(x)=g'(x)+h'(x)=3\\sec (3x) \\tan (3x) -2.\\]\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align*}\nm=f'(0) & =3\\sec\\left(3\\cdot 0\\right)\\tan\\left(3\\cdot 0\\right) -2\\\\[3pt]\n\t& =3\\sec 0\\tan 0 -2\\\\[3pt]\n\t& =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 - 2\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-2.       \n\\end{align*}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1&=m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1&=-2 \\left(x-0 \\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1&=-2x\\\\[3pt]\ny&=-2x+1.\n\\end{align}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260563 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd715637-0bbc-5207-bc3d-d4bc9fb07d3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272340} {:answer/id #uuid "e16c2e61-3120-579d-9039-62f41ff70545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272341} {:answer/id #uuid "94c7b1ec-fe6d-5b96-9d80-3876dc8f36c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272342} {:answer/id #uuid "29fc1319-8805-573e-9b33-6dfde4adf2d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272343} {:answer/id #uuid "3960cf73-b4ba-51a2-9f70-f5484a2a8248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3960cf73-b4ba-51a2-9f70-f5484a2a8248" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272344} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fecf18b-2bcc-5b78-b783-eca4bb55ef8a" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274748}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57478b30-a73d-57e4-8308-8dc05fea241e" :question/math-academy-id "q-48636" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x) = \\cos{\\left(x^3\\right)}$ at the point $\\left(0,1\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = x^3.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =3x^2.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= -\\sin{u} \\cdot 3x^2\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=-3x^2\\sin(x^3).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\begin{align}\nm=f'(0) & = -3(0)^2\\sin(0^3) = 0.\n\\end{align}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\\begin{align}\ny - y_1 & = m(x - x_1)\\\\[3pt]\n\ny - 1 & = 0 \\cdot \\left(x - 0 \\right)\\\\[3pt]\n\ny - 1 & = 0\\\\[3pt]\n\ny & =1.\n\\end{align}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475752a5-5f97-5b78-8ff9-860559774a3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272345} {:answer/id #uuid "acb943c7-2cc3-57c9-be82-6890f1c70fa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272346} {:answer/id #uuid "18be6e3e-1916-5880-9251-18c2c021ab4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272347} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc538a4-2cba-5dec-baf1-741acb00771b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272348} {:answer/id #uuid "4d04dc8a-4e41-5887-8c7d-278d064e7bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d04dc8a-4e41-5887-8c7d-278d064e7bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272349} :answer-field/id #uuid "af624237-a7a4-5995-bf0f-a5b04368ea03" :answer-field/key "y" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3eae5d22-d1dc-5383-9678-4b5348d7df9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y =\\cos \\left(\\sin x \\right)$ at the point $\\left(0,1 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 0$ is given by $f'(0).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cos{u}, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\sin{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =\\cos x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\sin u \\cdot (\\cos x)\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\cos x \\sin (\\sin x).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we find\n\\[\n\t\\eqalign{\n\tf'(0)&=-\\cos (0) \\sin (\\sin (0))\\\\[3pt]\n\t&=-1 \\cdot 0 \\\\[3pt]\n        &=0.\n}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,1) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[3pt]\ny-1 &= (0)\\cdot\\left(x-0\\right)\\\\[3pt]\ny&=1.\n\\end{align}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea24c558-8664-5b22-bdfc-71189a45a360" :knowledge-point/title "Scalar Multiplication of a Convergent Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aca859f-3151-568b-9bc0-16d15d8141f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[-3,\\infty)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272435} {:answer/id #uuid "c35358d5-9620-5e05-a527-a94cb78a1270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K < 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272436} {:answer/id #uuid "b196cf8d-bdb8-5922-a8ac-4905b2416e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272437} {:answer/id #uuid "eddb6bea-d9c0-5d32-a258-dd57d453911f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272438} {:answer/id #uuid "338da1b9-9ac2-5176-a864-acc7ef929d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b196cf8d-bdb8-5922-a8ac-4905b2416e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "K\\in\\mathbb R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272437} :answer-field/id #uuid "00954025-b037-58be-b8e7-0cd15acb7d9f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79b3da5f-c66e-5a1e-bee9-a95c92685b9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49824" :question/problem "Given that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent, what is the condition for the real constant $K$ guaranteeing that $K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent?" :question/worked-solution "Any constant multiple of a convergent series gives a convergent series. \n\nSo, $K\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is convergent for all $K.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260660 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "623fb477-441c-5573-8299-5f036006be51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272445} {:answer/id #uuid "98a6d6d6-6533-534d-8518-39cc168967d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q > 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272446} {:answer/id #uuid "03cd4963-9ab5-5eac-98da-734e3762cfc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272447} {:answer/id #uuid "70138a6b-2542-5001-b479-ee1fbcfcbcf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < (1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272448} {:answer/id #uuid "81c332e4-fb28-540d-8ca7-8f68cb78e65c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "623fb477-441c-5573-8299-5f036006be51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272445} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7b7f3c-9be0-5d12-be6a-ea1296d0210d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274769}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "485f013d-0bc9-5843-bf53-dbb0e9f37daa" :question/math-academy-id "q-50450" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3q-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=3q-1.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n3q-1>1\\,⟹\\,q>\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>\\frac{2}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c95da0-1173-5ee0-b587-6cf95807f516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q > 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272450} {:answer/id #uuid "a78be9cc-11ec-5851-8cb8-667764a64c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272451} {:answer/id #uuid "9410baa5-6cbd-5aee-853e-fa1c2d3f29e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272452} {:answer/id #uuid "fc046b7e-dfb7-5afa-83d3-6e022d2f019f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < (1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272453} {:answer/id #uuid "59101bab-b698-5d33-8812-9e585baa703d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a78be9cc-11ec-5851-8cb8-667764a64c2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272451} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77f806b-bae1-5176-afe7-1bdf2ee565fd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274770}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "256094b3-ea33-541d-8e90-d5daa58588f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-50621" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2q-2}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=2q-2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n2q-2>1\\,⟹\\,q>\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4dff2ea-a90b-5618-82fc-647a40be0c46" :knowledge-point/title "Convergence Parameters for p-Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aba4582b-078d-593e-bee1-d0aeceebff9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272455} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c873c4-3f1c-5ac1-84a7-30f5061ae5bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q > 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272456} {:answer/id #uuid "62c5dbfc-1972-5c31-be8d-304a0af27e4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272457} {:answer/id #uuid "67bec40f-ef63-5190-9ad1-16eea984fbf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < (1)/(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272458} {:answer/id #uuid "dbf155e4-09bc-5f80-aa3e-db7f534e5be1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q < 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aba4582b-078d-593e-bee1-d0aeceebff9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q>\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272455} :answer-field/id #uuid "92e73fbd-5d43-5739-b367-e5ef7b7ae84b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274771}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35266cb0-18e1-5e7d-94b2-eb7099779b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-50623" :question/problem "For which values of $q$ does the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{4q-1}}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "A $p$-series is any series that takes the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{p}},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. The $p$-series test states that\n\n- if $p>1,$ then the series converges, while\n\n- if $p\\le 1,$ then the series diverges.\n\nComparing our series to the $p$-series, we have\n\n$$\np=4q-1.\n$$\n\nAccording to the $p$-series test, the given series converges if\n\n$$\n4q-1>1\\,⟹\\,q>\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent for all $q>\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f58e5-0f98-58b1-b2f7-9f0ee33ed075" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f85e470-654a-53ba-9c3f-664ad6b83365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272689} {:answer/id #uuid "1abf42bd-f0b1-56fc-9f69-e319d8720ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272690} {:answer/id #uuid "4debf9bf-68be-5ae3-a480-e067270abc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272691} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc00ced-38ed-5ae7-9764-c94021da22bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272692} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc114c3-f8c5-5ab4-8dd4-df1de22e8805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answ